# G090 — Zip File Maker

*v2. See `zip-file-maker.md` for prose: byte-level round-trip as
compression contract, RLE as minimal-interesting exemplar, per-entry
method selection, archive as manifest + payload.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Bundle

[vault-bundle @backup-name
    IN ORDER TO
        archive every note in the vault as individually-compressed entries
        pick the smaller of Store or RLE per file
        and write the serialised bundle to the backup directory

    @bundle <- [archive[new]]
    @notes  <- [vault[find {path: "*.md"}]]

    for @note in @notes
        @bytes <- [fs[read-bytes {path: @note:path}]]
        [archive[add-file
                   {archive: @bundle,
                    name: @note:path,
                    data: @bytes}]]

    @manifest <- [archive[manifest {archive: @bundle}]]
    @ratio    <- [archive[compression-ratio {archive: @bundle}]]

    [vault[save-bytes
            {path: "backups/" + @backup-name + ".zb",
             content: [archive[serialise {archive: @bundle}]]}]]

    [ui[show-bundle {manifest: @manifest, ratio: @ratio}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[rle
    encode          {data}
    decode          {encoded}                     ;; || truncated-input
]

[archive
    new
    add-file        {archive, name, data}          ;; picks Store or RLE
    add-stored      {archive, name, data}          ;; forces Store
    extract         {archive, name}                ;; || not-found
    names           {archive}
    manifest        {archive}
    compressed-size {archive}
    compression-ratio {archive}
    serialise       {archive}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: method selection as `where` smaller-wins

[archive[add-file {archive: @a, name: @n, data: @bytes}]
    IN ORDER TO
        store @bytes under @n using whichever method produces less output
        because a compressor that always compresses doubles random data

    @rle-size <- size of [rle[encode {data: @bytes}]]
    @bytes
        where @rle-size is less than original size
            -> store with method RLE
        otherwise
            -> store with method STORE (no transform)
]

The algorithm evaluates its own output. Same meta-pattern appears in
LSM compaction, JPEG quality tuning, and every adaptive system that
decides whether to transform at all.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: byte-identity as a `where` invariant

[archive[extract {archive: @a, name: @n}]
    IN ORDER TO
        return the exact bytes that were added under @n
        where [extract] ∘ [add-file] is the identity on byte sequences
        so no bit of the original file is lost
]

Textual round-trip (G085) could normalise whitespace; byte round-trip
cannot normalise anything. Binary fidelity is the compression
category's hard contract.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @a <- [archive[new]]

    [archive[add-file {archive: @a, name: "repetitive.txt",
                        data: "aaaaaaaaaa"}]]
    ;; -> method=RLE (10 bytes -> 2 bytes)

    [archive[add-file {archive: @a, name: "random.bin",
                        data: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]}]]
    ;; -> method=STORE (RLE would expand to 10; keep 5 original)

    [archive[add-file {archive: @a, name: "big.txt",
                        data: "x" repeated 1000 times}]]
    ;; -> method=RLE (1000 bytes -> 8 bytes, 4 runs of 255+235)

    -> [archive[extract {archive: @a, name: "repetitive.txt"}]]
    ;; -> "aaaaaaaaaa"

    -> [archive[compression-ratio {archive: @a}]]
    ;; -> ~0.04
]
